The amount by which the inventory balance of Halliburton Company (NYSE: HAL) would increase if the average cost method were used instead of LIFO as of June 30, 2019 is **$21 million**.

## Explanation

According to Note 6 (Inventories) of Halliburton's Q2 2019 Form 10-Q (filed July 26, 2019):

- Halliburton uses the **last-in, first-out (LIFO)** method for certain finished products and parts inventories for drill bits, completion products, bulk materials, and other tools in the United States. These LIFO-based inventories totaled **$189 million** as of June 30, 2019.
- The filing explicitly discloses: *"If the average cost method had been used, total inventories would have been **$21 million higher** than reported as of June 30, 2019."*
- For comparison, the same figure was $24 million as of December 31, 2018. The decline in this LIFO reserve (from $24 million to $21 million) reflects a partial liquidation of LIFO layers during the first half of 2019.

This $21 million represents what is often referred to as the "LIFO reserve" — the cumulative difference between LIFO inventory valuation and what the inventory would be valued at under the average cost method.

