## Constellation Energy (CEG) — 2022 Stock-Based Compensation Adjustment

The 2022 10-K filing for Constellation Energy provides two relevant figures for stock-based compensation:

### 1. Total Stock-Based Compensation Expense: **$116 million**
Per **Note 20 — Stock-Based Compensation Plans**, the total stock-based compensation expense recognized in the Consolidated Statements of Operations for the year ended December 31, 2022, was **$116 million** (pre-tax), with an after-tax expense of $87 million.

### 2. Cash Flow Adjustment: **$44 million**
On the **Consolidated Statements of Cash Flows**, there is no separate line item for stock-based compensation. Instead, it is included within "Other non-cash operating activities" ($249 million for 2022). **Note 23 — Supplemental Financial Information** provides a breakdown of this line item and lists **"Long-term incentive plan"** at **$44 million** for 2022. This is the non-cash, equity-settled portion of stock-based compensation that is added back as an adjustment to reconcile net income to net cash provided by operating activities.

### Explanation of the Difference
The $116 million total expense includes both equity-settled awards (e.g., restricted stock units and the stock portion of performance share awards) and cash-settled liability awards (e.g., the 50% cash portion of performance share awards). Only the equity-settled portion ($44 million) represents a true non-cash item requiring a cash flow adjustment. The remaining ~$72 million relates to cash-settled components and/or other items that do not appear as a non-cash adjustment.

### Answer
The **2022 adjustment figure** for Constellation Energy's Stock-based Compensation Expense — i.e., the amount added back on the cash flow statement as a non-cash item — is **$44 million**.

