## Maximum Potential Discount Percentage: **10.00%**

### Reasoning

The question references JPMorgan structured notes (Digital Barrier Notes, CUSIP 46660MBF1), issued by JPMorgan Chase Financial Company LLC and fully and unconditionally guaranteed by JPMorgan Chase & Co. (NYSE: JPM), as detailed in a preliminary pricing supplement (Form 424B2) filed on February 27, 2026.

The key disclosure from the filing states:

> "The estimated value of the notes, when the terms of the notes are set, will be provided in the pricing supplement and **will not be less than $900.00 per $1,000 principal amount note.**"

The notes are offered to the public at a **Price to Public of $1,000.00** per $1,000 principal amount.

The maximum potential discount from the principal amount at issuance is the difference between the Price to Public ($1,000) and the Estimated Value floor ($900):

**Maximum Potential Discount = ($1,000.00 − $900.00) / $1,000.00 = 10.00%**

This 10% maximum potential discount reflects the combined impact of selling commissions, projected hedging profits, and other costs embedded in the original issue price that cause the estimated value to be lower than the price paid by investors.

