Based on a comprehensive review of Mastercard's SEC filings, including the 2022 10-K (filed February 14, 2023), the 2021 10-K, quarterly 10-Q filings for 2022, and the 2023 proxy statement, **Mastercard did not make any specific adjustment to its Stock-based Compensation Expense in 2022.**

The stock-based compensation expense for the year ended December 31, 2022, was **$295 million**, as reported in Note 18 (Share-Based Payments) of the 2022 10-K. This compares to $273 million in 2021 and $254 million in 2020.

The only adjustment related to stock-based compensation mentioned in the 2022 10-K was a modification that occurred **in 2020**, not 2022. Specifically, during the year ended December 31, 2020, performance targets related to PSU awards granted in 2018 were adjusted to exclude certain pandemic-related financial impacts. This 2020 adjustment required modification accounting but was described as having an "immaterial impact on compensation expense." No mention of any continuing impact from this modification in 2022 was found.

I also searched for terms such as "true-up," "catch-up," "remeasurement," "forfeiture adjustment," and "modification" across all filings, and none appeared in the context of stock-based compensation for 2022. The $295 million expense was the normal recurring expense calculated using the company's straight-line attribution method, net of estimated forfeitures, with no special adjustments disclosed.

If the question is interpreted as the year-over-year change in stock-based compensation expense (from 2021 to 2022), that increase was:
- **$295 million - $273 million = $22 million** (an increase of approximately 8.06%).

However, the filings do not characterize this as an "adjustment" — it is simply the difference in the annual expense figures.

