The maximum share dilution that Southwest Airlines' convertible notes could cause as of December 31, 2022 is **41.87 million shares** (or more precisely, 41,871,339.9 shares).

## Calculation:

Based on Southwest Airlines' 2022 10-K filing (Note 7 - Financing Activities):

- **Principal amount of 1.25% Convertible Senior Notes due 2025 outstanding as of December 31, 2022:** $1,611 million ($1,611,000,000)
- **Conversion rate as of December 31, 2022:** 25.9909 shares of common stock per $1,000 principal amount

**Maximum shares issuable upon full conversion:**
$$\frac{\$1,611,000,000}{\$1,000} \times 25.9909 = 1,611,000 \times 25.9909 = 41,871,339.9 \text{ shares}$$

This represents approximately **41.87 million shares**.

## Context:

- **Shares outstanding as of December 31, 2022:** 593,999,821 shares (from Consolidated Balance Sheet)
- **Dilution percentage:** 41,871,339.9 / (593,999,821 + 41,871,339.9) = **6.58%** of post-conversion total share count

- **Settlement policy:** The Company intends to settle conversions by paying cash up to the principal amount, with any excess conversion value settled in cash or shares. However, the maximum theoretical dilution assumes full share settlement.

- **Conversion status:** As of December 31, 2022, the Convertible Notes did not meet the criteria to be converted by holders (the if-converted value was less than the principal amount by $201 million).

- **Note:** The diluted EPS calculation for 2022 included 47 million shares related to convertible notes, but this is a weighted average for the entire year (when the principal balance was higher at the beginning of the year before repurchases).

